// Rounds `stwPollOn`'s locked section retries a failed `worldRendezvous`
// before truly abandoning the collection (#5322). Each round only has to
// gather whatever stragglers the round before it did not — `worldRendezvous`
// reuses the same epoch across rounds (see its own comment,
// ../gc/gcworldstop.bit), so a round never re-collects an ack a prior round
// already banked. Bounded (Power-of-10 rule 2), and sized generously against
// the worst contention #5322 measured (up to 20 of 34 threads see comment 2
// on that ticket) while still finite: a slot that genuinely never reaches a
// safepoint (a leaked-registration bug, not contention) surfaces as an
// abandoned, counted collection within a bounded number of rounds rather than
// hanging forever.
const stwRendezvousRetryBound = 64

// The wall-clock ceiling on the WHOLE retry, all rounds together (#6423).
//
// The rounds above are bounded in spins, and a spin count is a bound in CPU
// throughput, not in time: with one mutator that never reaches a safepoint (a
// thread inside a kernel call no `gcBlockingBegin`/`gcBlockingEnd` brackets,
// ABI.md section 5) the 64 rounds took 360 ms on an idle arm64 box and 20-24 s
// beside 36 CPU hogs, with every other thread parked for all of it (#6412).
// This turns that into a time bound that does not stretch with load.
//
// THE FIGURE IS MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. It has to sit above how long a mutator
// that is merely descheduled, and live, needs to reach its next safepoint
// under heavy oversubscription, because below that an ordinary collection is
// abandoned for no reason (#2415's failure, `_tests_/stress/gcslotexitdarwin`
// at 28 concurrent instances). See the ticket for the sweep it was read from.
const stwRendezvousCeilingNs = 250000000

// The retry loop itself, split out of `stwPollOn` so that function's own
// complexity stays at its pre-#5322 count — see the call site's comment for
// the mechanism this replaces. Same collector, same collector lock, held by
// the caller for the whole loop: no other thread can start a competing
// collection while this retries, which is what keeps a losing round from
// turning into a fresh collector election instead of a continuation of this
// one.
//
// THE STOP FLAG STAYS SET BETWEEN ROUNDS, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE (#6423).
// Releasing it between rounds would let unrelated mutators run while the
// collector waits for a straggler, but it cannot be done soundly without
// giving back what #5322 fixed: `worldRestart` wakes every parked thread, each
// of which then races for the collector lock, and the next round's fresh
// stop bumps the epoch, which un-stops every slot that had already acked the
// old one. That is the livelock (epoch climbing, collections frozen). Holding
// the stop is what makes a round only gather the stragglers. The cost, every
// other thread parked for the whole retry, is bounded by the ceiling above
// rather than avoided, and `stwAfterAbandon` (./stwstats.bit) keeps the world
// from paying it again at once.
//
// On giving up, `worldStuckNote` (../gc/gcworldstuck.bit) names the slot that
// never answered; `stwFinishPause` prints it after the world is restarted.
@nosplit fn stwRetryRendezvous(world: *i64, selfSlot: int): bool {
  let t0 = gcTicks()
  let deadline = t0 + stwRendezvousCeilingNs
  let round = 0
  while (round < stwRendezvousRetryBound) {
    if (worldRendezvous(world, selfSlot, deadline)) {
      return true
    }
    if (gcTicks() >= deadline) {
      break
    }
    round = round + 1
  }
  worldStuckNote(world, selfSlot, gcTicks() - t0)
  return false
}
